## Why this matters

The capstone combines partitioning, replication and storage into one testable model of a distributed key-value service. The deliverable is not a production database; it is a specification of what the service guarantees, an executable model small enough to trace by hand, and failure traces that show the guarantee holding or, honestly, where it does not. Combining familiar components does not define a consistency guarantee; writing the traces does.

## The simple version

Think of the store as a post office with several branches. Consistent hashing decides which branches hold a copy of each letter; replication keeps N copies; quorums decide how many branches must confirm a write (W) and how many must be asked on a read (R) before the office answers. If W plus R exceeds N, every read overlaps at least one branch that saw the latest write. The analogy breaks at "latest": branches can hold different versions of the same letter after a partition, so each copy carries a version, and the office must decide how to reconcile disagreement instead of hoping it never happens.

## The lab

Run the setup in an isolated directory. It creates a Node project with a model skeleton and a trace runner; nothing outside the temporary directory is touched.

```bash
lab=$(mktemp -d) && cd "$lab" || exit 1
mkdir -p src traces
cat > src/model.mjs <<'JS'
// A single-process model: nodes are objects; the network is a function you can make fail.
export function makeCluster(names, { n = 3, w = 2, r = 2 } = {}) {
  const nodes = new Map(names.map((name) => [name, { name, up: true, data: new Map(), log: [] }]));
  const ring = [...names].sort();
  const owners = (key) => { const start = [...key].reduce((h, c) => (h * 31 + c.charCodeAt(0)) % ring.length, 0); return Array.from({ length: n }, (_, i) => nodes.get(ring[(start + i) % ring.length])); };
  const put = (key, value, version) => {
    const acks = owners(key).filter((node) => node.up).map((node) => { node.data.set(key, { value, version }); node.log.push({ key, value, version }); return node.name; });
    if (acks.length < w) throw new Error(`write needs ${w} acks, got ${acks.length}`);
    return acks;
  };
  const get = (key) => {
    const replies = owners(key).filter((node) => node.up).map((node) => node.data.get(key)).filter(Boolean);
    if (replies.length < r) throw new Error(`read needs ${r} replies, got ${replies.length}`);
    return replies.reduce((best, cur) => (!best || cur.version > best.version ? cur : best), null);
  };
  return { nodes, owners, put, get };
}
JS
printf 'lab at %s\n' "$lab"
```

## Core mechanics

Replica placement: the first N owners on the sorted ring hold each key; adding or removing a node moves only the keys whose owner set changed. Versioned writes: every write carries a version (a counter per key or a vector clock), and reads return the highest version among R replies, then repair stale replicas with it. Log recovery: a node that rejoins replays its log against the current owners and accepts newer versions from them before it serves reads. The consistency specification states, for the chosen N, W and R, which of the models from the course the service provides for a single key (for W + R > N and a single writer: read-your-writes and monotonic reads; without a coordinator it does not provide linearizability across concurrent writers) and what a client must do about concurrent versions.

## Runnable experiment

Predict each line, then run it with `node` from the lab root.

```js
import { makeCluster } from './src/model.mjs';
const c = makeCluster(['a', 'b', 'c', 'd'], { n: 3, w: 2, r: 2 });
console.log(c.owners('user:1').map((x) => x.name));  // three names from the ring
console.log(c.put('user:1', 'v1', 1));               // three acks
c.owners('user:1')[0].up = false;                     // one owner leaves during the next write
console.log(c.put('user:1', 'v2', 2));               // two acks: the write still meets W
console.log(c.get('user:1').value);                   // v2: R=2 overlaps a replica that has version 2
c.owners('user:1')[0].up = true;                      // the node rejoins with stale data
console.log(c.owners('user:1')[0].data.get('user:1')); // { value: 'v1', version: 1 }: stale until recovered
```

Write the recovery step yourself: on rejoin, the node asks its co-owners for their versions and keeps the highest, then re-run the last line.

## Failure modes

Choosing W = 1 and R = 1 for speed and calling the store "consistent" hides that reads can miss writes. Reconciling by wall-clock timestamps loses writes when clocks skew. Letting a rejoining node serve reads before recovery returns stale data with a valid-looking version. Moving all keys when one node joins defeats the point of the ring. A specification that says "eventually consistent" without stating the session guarantees leaves the application unable to reason about its own reads.

## Practical exercise

Deliver the model and its specification: `src/model.mjs` with versioned writes, quorum reads with read repair, recovery on rejoin and a stable ring; `traces/` with at least three executable failure traces (an owner leaving mid-write, a node rejoining stale, a partition that splits the owners) each asserting the guarantee or documenting the violation; and `SPEC.md` stating N, W, R, the single-key guarantees provided and not provided, the reconciliation rule for concurrent versions, and the recovery protocol.

exercise.starter_code:
// traces/rejoin.test.mjs (sketch)
import test from 'node:test';
import assert from 'node:assert/strict';
import { makeCluster } from '../src/model.mjs';
test('a rejoining node recovers the newest version before serving', () => {
  const c = makeCluster(['a', 'b', 'c', 'd']);
  c.put('k', 'v1', 1);
  const [stale] = c.owners('k'); stale.up = false;
  c.put('k', 'v2', 2);
  stale.up = true;
  c.recover(stale);  // you write this
  assert.equal(stale.data.get('k').version, 2);
});
